    {
      "type": "tweet",
      "id": "1952259294164136149",
      "url": "https://x.com/RickelKendra/status/1952259294164136149",
      "twitterUrl": "https://twitter.com/RickelKendra/status/1952259294164136149",
      "text": "Behind Dubai’s glittering skyline, luxury malls, and futuristic towers lies a darker, hidden city—one built not by visionaries, but by invisible labor. The world sees Burj Khalifa, artificial islands, and opulence, but rarely sees the workers who made it all possible. These are the men and women shipped in from Bangladesh, India, Pakistan, Nepal, and the Philippines—promised jobs and a better life, only to find themselves trapped in a system that exploits their bodies and erases their dignity.\n\nThey are bused in at dawn from labor camps on the outskirts of the city—massive, prison-like compounds hidden from tourists and the elite. These workers often live eight or more to a room, with bunk beds stacked in sweltering, cramped quarters. Many have their passports confiscated upon arrival. They cannot leave without permission. They are not allowed to unionize or protest. In many cases, their wages are delayed or cut without explanation. This is not just exploitation—it is a system of indentured servitude dressed in the clothes of modern development.\n\nThe same applies to taxi drivers, nannies, construction workers, and cleaners. Taxi drivers work 12–16 hour shifts just to make enough to send a few dollars home. They sleep in communal apartments, sometimes hot-bedding in shifts. They're fined for minor infractions, and their income is tightly controlled. They are surveilled, squeezed, and forgotten. Yet without them, Dubai would cease to function. They are the gears turning beneath the golden facade.\n\nThe UAE’s Kafala system—where a worker’s legal status is tied to their employer—creates conditions ripe for abuse. It's legal serfdom, operating with a smile. Any attempt to resist or escape can result in detention, deportation, or worse. Meanwhile, the country markets itself as a global hub of innovation, culture, and prosperity—ignoring the very people who built the dream.\n\nThis isn't just a labor issue. It's a moral one. The global elite come to Dubai to escape taxes, flaunt wealth, and indulge in extravagance—oblivious or indifferent to the human toll. The architecture is stunning, but the foundation is stained. No amount of glass, steel, or imported luxury can hide the fact that this city was built on the backs of those who had no choice.\n\nDubai is not alone—many modern cities rely on hidden exploitation. But its contrast is stark: a hypermodern Disneyland for the rich resting on the daily exhaustion of a working class made invisible by design.\n\nIf justice means anything, it must include the hands that build our cities—not just the ones that profit from them.",
