    {
      "type": "tweet",
      "id": "1952263955646194060",
      "url": "https://x.com/ZackEiseman/status/1952263955646194060",
      "twitterUrl": "https://twitter.com/ZackEiseman/status/1952263955646194060",
      "text": "ICYMI O/N & WEEKEND\n\nTRADE:\n\nUSTR Greer said that tariffs Trump imposed last week on scores of countries are likely to stay in place rather than be cut as part of continuing negotiations. Greer also said recent trade talks with Beijing had been \"very positive\" and were focused on the supply of rare earth magnets and minerals. (CBS)\n\nChina is limiting the flow of critical minerals to Western defense manufacturers, delaying production and forcing companies to scour the world for stockpiles of the minerals needed to make everything from bullets to jet fighters. (WSJ)\n\nChinese exporters are reconsidering investment in offshore factories as US tariffs on alternative hubs and new restrictions on “transshipment” force a sweeping rethink of supply chains in Asia. (FT)\n\nThe Swiss government is open to revising its offer to the US in response to planned heavy tariffs, Business Minister Guy Parmelin said, as experts warned the 39% import duties announced by Trump could trigger a recession in Switzerland. (RTRS)\n\nJapanese PM Ishiba said on Monday he will not hesitate to hold talks with US President Trump to ensure an agreed cut to U.S. automobile tariffs is implemented soon. (RTRS)\n\nTrump and Canadian PM Carney will likely talk \"over the next number of days\" after the U.S. imposed a 35% tariff on goods not covered by the U.S.-Mexico-Canada trade agreement, a Canadian official said on Sunday. (RTRS)\n\nSouth Korea’s presidential policy chief said the country’s $350 billion investment pledge as part of the US trade deal is largely structured as loan guarantees rather than direct capital injections, seeking to ease domestic concerns over the scale and risks of the agreement. (BBG)\n\nThe Philippines may temporarily suspend rice imports to support local farmers in the world’s biggest buyer, potentially increasing the risk of expanding a global surplus. (BBG)\n\nMACRO:\n\nTrump said on Friday that Fed Chair Powell will \"most likely\" stay in his position. Trump said he would remove Powell \"in a heartbeat\" and said the Fed's interest rate was too high but added that others have said Powell's removal would \"disturb the market.\" (Newxmax)\n\nTrump said he will announce a new head of BLS in the next three or four days. (RTRS)\n\nWhite House Economic Advisor Hassett said that the firing of BLS commissioner Erika McEntarfer is part of Trump’s plan to make further changes to the agency. (NBC)\n\nTrump said he is to announce replacement for Fed's Kugler in the next couple of days. (RTRS)\n\nFed's Williams said that he would go into the September policy meeting with \"very much an open mind\" about cutting interest rates. (WSJ)\n\nOPEC+ agreed on Sunday to raise oil production by 547,000 barrels per day for September, the latest in a series of accelerated output hikes to regain market share, as concerns mount over potential supply disruptions linked to Russia. (RTRS)\n\nJapanese PM Ishiba said on Monday the government is ready to compile an extra budget to cushion the economic blow from U.S. tariffs. Some analysts expect it could reach around 10 trillion yen ($67.68 billion), which would require additional debt issuance. (RTRS)\n\nChina will likely step up monetary easing efforts in the second half of this year by cutting benchmark interest rates and banks’ reserve requirements in order to guide overall financing costs lower and support the economy, according to analysts in China press. (Shanghai Securities News).\n\nChina said it plans to tax interest income on bonds issued by the government and financial institutions, in a surprise move that’s prompted investors to reevaluate their debt market positions. (BBG)\n\nChina’s local governments including Guangzhou are actively studying plans to tap the nation’s “new financing policy tool” to fund infrastructure investment. (China Securities Journal)\n\nCopper rose for a second day as Chilean mining giant Codelco grappled with the aftermath of a deadly accident at one of the world’s biggest underground mines. (BBG)\n\nSouth Korean stocks rose the most in Asia on Monday as opposition to a plan to hike tax on capital gains intensified. (BBG)\n\nIran and Iraq are close to resolving a dispute over disruptions to Iranian gas supplies and Baghdad’s unpaid dues for the exports, Iran’s Oil Minister Mohsen Paknejad said Saturday. (BBG)\n\nGEOPOLITICAL:\n\nTrump said there will be sanctions if Russia does not stop the war, but that Russia seems to be good at avoiding sanctions, adding that special envoy Witkoff will be going to Russia on Wednesday or Thursday. (RTRS)\n\nIndia will keep purchasing oil from Russia despite Trump's threats of penalties, two Indian government sources told Reuters on Saturday. (RTRS)\n\nWhite House Deputy Chief of Staff Stephen Miller on Sunday accused India of effectively financing Russia's war in Ukraine by purchasing oil from Moscow, after Trump escalated pressure on New Delhi to stop buying Russian oil. (RTRS)\n\n'Home by Home, Russia Is Selling Occupied Ukraine to Russians' WSJ article notes Kremlin-linked construction firms reap profits from redevelopment of housing destroyed by Russia’s invasion. (WSJ)\n\nEQUITIES:\n\nBerkshire Hathaway said on Saturday it took a $3.76 billion write-down on its stake in Kraft Heinz during the second quarter, an acknowledgment the decade-old investment hasn't worked out. (WSJ)\n\nAmphenol is nearing a deal to buy CommScope Holding’s broadband connectivity and cable unit, according to WJS sources. The deal would be valued at roughly $10.5 billion, including debt. (WSJ)\n\nMore than 3,200 union members who assemble Boeing's fighter jets in the St. Louis area and Illinois went on strike on Monday after rejecting a second contract offer the previous day. (RTRS)\n\nUS railroad customer groups have demanded regulators block or put onerous conditions on the proposed merger of Union Pacific and Norfolk Southern. (FT)\n\nMeta has placed big orders to Taiwan's Quanta Computer for customized AI servers based on its next-generation ASIC (own chip, made with Broadcom). The order is seen at up to 6,000 cabinets, shipped by year-end. (UDN via @dnystedt)\n\nXiaomi on Monday released an open-source voice model to complement its automotive and home appliance technologies, further heating up the race to build AI tools for more than just text. (BBG)\n\nChina Mobile, the world's biggest wireless carrier by users, said on Monday it had bought a 14.4% stake in Hong Kong-based broadband service provider HKBN for HK$1.08 billion ($137.58 million). (RTRS)\n\nUBS said it reached an agreement with the U.S. Justice Department to pay $300 million to resolve outstanding consumer-relief obligations Credit Suisse committed to. (WSJ)\n\nDATA RECAP (Newsquawk)\n\nAustralian Melbourne Institute Inflation Gauge MM (Jul) 0.9% (Prev. 0.1%)\nAustralian Melbourne Institute Inflation Gauge YY (Jul) 2.9% (Prev. 2.4%)\nSwiss CPI MM (Jul) 0.0% vs. Exp. -0.2% (Prev. 0.2%)\nSwiss CPI YY (Jul) 0.2% vs. Exp. 0.1% (Prev. 0.1%)\nTurkish CPI MM* (Jul) 2.06% vs. Exp. 2.4% (Prev. 1.37%)\nTurkish CPI YY* (Jul) 33.52% vs. Exp. 34.05% (Prev. 35.05%)",
